# adaptive-continuation-over-stoppage

When exact tools, ideally organized resources, or perfect conditions are unavailable, apply a documented workaround and continue work rather than halting. Stoppage is only appropriate when a hard safety or specification limit would be violated, not merely because conditions are inconvenient or suboptimal.

## Procedures
- Identify the gap between available resource and ideal resource.
- Check whether the gap violates a hard safety or specification limit; if so, escalate rather than continuing.
- If no hard limit is violated, select the nearest acceptable substitute (e.g., next wrench size up, workaround protocol) and apply it.
- Document the substitution (tool size, deviation, workaround used) before proceeding.
- Continue the primary workflow without restart.

## Conventions
- Workaround must be documented at the point of application, not retroactively.
- Never stop and wait for ideal conditions when an acceptable substitute exists.

